@node Command Line
@section Command Line

@cindex Command Line

The Python file @code{counterpart.py} implements most of the reference conversion for @strong{Counterpart}. 

This file can be run as a stand-alone command line utility to open a Texinfo web page URL in Emacs. In this way@comma{} @strong{Counterpart} can also be used on a Linux system as this approach does not require macOS nor Safari.

@subsubheading Command Line Usage

The basic usage is to run @code{counterpart} with the @code{--no-shortcut} and @code{-p} (or @code{--path}) options:

@example
$ python counterpart.py --no-shortcut -p '/snap/bin' <reference>
@end example

The above invocation will generate the translated @code{<reference>} to an Info node reference.

The file @code{counterpart.py} can be made executable by copying it to a directory in the @code{PATH} environment variable and turning on its file mode to executable via @code{chmod}. It is common to rename the file to have no suffix as well.


@subsubheading Command Line Help

Running @code{counterpart} with the @code{-h} or @code{--help} option will show its usage string.

@example
usage: counterpart [-h] [-v] [-s | --shortcut | --no-shortcut] [-e | --execute | --no-execute] [-p PATH] [-t @{scrim@comma{}info@}] reference

Convert Texinfo web page reference to Info node.

positional arguments:
  reference             Either URL path or URL.

options:
  -h@comma{} --help            show this help message and exit
  -v@comma{} --version         print version information and exit
  -s@comma{} --shortcut@comma{} --no-shortcut
                        If set@comma{} run as if invoked via macOS Shortcut.
  -e@comma{} --execute@comma{} --no-execute
                        Open counterpart reference. Only valid when --no-shortcut is used.
  -p@comma{} --path PATH       Path to emacsclient. Only valid when --no-shortcut is used.
  -t@comma{} --target @{scrim@comma{}info@}
                        Target format. Only valid when --no-shortcut is used.
@end example




@subsubheading Examples

Here are some examples of running @code{counterpart}.

Write Info node reference to @code{stdout} from counterpart web page URL:

@example
$ counterpart --no-shortcut -p '/snap/bin' 'https://kickingvegas.github.io/triode/Features.html'
(info "(triode) Features")
@end example

Write Info node reference to @code{stdout} from counterpart web page URL and open in Emacs via @code{emacsclient}:

@example
$ counterpart --no-shortcut --execute -p '/snap/bin' 'https://kickingvegas.github.io/triode/Features.html'
@end example

